From 416025a76492e12308dad58b84d921a77eb758f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pugant <pugant@localhost>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:18:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 04/19] save-stream-CRC: fold the persist CRC while writing, no
 tmp read-back [w6-7]

W6-7 (A3-3). The persist/park save used to compute the sidecar CRC by
re-reading the whole tmp file with llama_persist_crc32_file right after
fdatasync + POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED had dropped it from the page cache - a
full cold read of 0.9-2.3 GB per save purely to hash bytes the process
had just written. state_seq_save_file() now takes an optional crc_out:
the CRC-32 is folded over the exact bytes handed to write_raw (header
and token section first, then the state section through the io writer's
single write() funnel), and the new public llama_state_seq_save_file_crc()
exposes it. save_disk passes the sidecar CRC sink for both components and
drops the post-fsync read-back; the size_exact stat still guards the
on-disk length, so an unreadable/short/long file still fails the save.

Gate N: test-state-io-buffer proves the stream-CRC save produces a file
byte-identical to the plain save (and to the pre-change golden artifacts)
and that the folded CRC equals a whole-file read-back CRC; crc_out == NULL
keeps the plain-save behavior exactly.

Note: bytes that corrupt after a successful write+fsync are no longer
detected at save time (the read-back pass doubled as a read verification);
the restore-time CRC still catches them before any context mutation.

Co-Authored-By: GLM by z.ai
